---
import Footer from "../../components/shared/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../../components/shared/Header.astro";
import PageHeader from "../../components/shared/PageHeader.astro";
import ContactBanner from "../../components/shared/ContactBanner.astro";
import LeadBlock from "../../components/shared/LeadBlock.astro";
import Icon from "../../components/shared/Icon.astro";
import WtTrackerDiagram from "../../components/case-studies/WtTrackerDiagram.astro";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { organization, siteUrl } from "../../data/organization";

const schema = [
  organization,
  {
    "@type": "SoftwareSourceCode",
    name: "wt-tracker",
    description:
      "High-performance WebTorrent tracker for WebRTC signaling in P2P networks, built on Node.js and µWebSockets.js.",
    url: `${siteUrl}case-studies/wt-tracker`,
    codeRepository: "https://github.com/novage/wt-tracker",
    programmingLanguage: "TypeScript",
    license: "https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0",
    author: { "@id": organization["@id"] },
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="wt-tracker: High-Performance WebTorrent Tracker | Novage"
  canonical="case-studies/wt-tracker"
  schema={schema}
  description="How we built wt-tracker, an open-source WebTorrent tracker on Node.js and µWebSockets.js that handles 20,000 peers on a VPS with 1 vCPU and 2 GiB of memory."
>
  <Header />
  <div id="content">
    <article>
      <PageHeader
        eyebrow="Case study"
        title="wt-tracker"
        accent="High-Performance WebTorrent Tracker"
      >
        <p class="lead">
          How we built a Node.js WebSocket server that handles{" "}
          <strong>20,000 simultaneous peers</strong> on a VPS with only 1
          virtual CPU and 2 GiB of memory — and why that matters for the cost of
          P2P video.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button primary" href="https://github.com/novage/wt-tracker">
            <Icon name="github" class="inline-icon" />
            View on GitHub
          </a>
          <a
            class="button secondary"
            href="#contact"
            data-umami-event="wt-tracker-discuss"
          >
            Discuss your project
          </a>
        </div>
      </PageHeader>

      <div class="page container content">
        <ul class="key-facts">
          <li>
            <strong>20,000</strong> simultaneous WebSocket Secure peers
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>1 vCPU, 2 GiB</strong> a single small VPS
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>0</strong> load balancers or clusters needed
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Apache-2.0</strong> open source
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The challenge</h2>
        <p>
          Viewers in a <a href="/p2p-media-loader/overview">P2P Media Loader</a>{" "}
          network find each other through a WebTorrent tracker. Every viewer
          keeps a WebSocket connection open to the tracker, announces the
          streams it is watching, and exchanges WebRTC connection offers and
          answers (SDP) with other viewers of the same stream — a process called
          WebRTC signaling.
        </p>
        <p>
          Public trackers are fine to start with, but a production audience
          needs its own. The load is unusual: tens of thousands of long-lived
          TLS connections and a constant flow of small JSON messages, where
          every announce can fan out into offers to many other peers. The
          reference implementation is built on the standard Node.js networking
          stack, which spends a lot of CPU and memory per connection at that
          scale.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our goal was to serve a large audience from the cheapest possible
          server: one small VPS, with no cluster and no load balancer.
        </p>

        <h2>How we made it fast</h2>

        <figure class="diagram-figure">
          <WtTrackerDiagram />
          <figcaption>
            Every peer keeps a WebSocket connection open. µWebSockets.js handles
            the networking natively, and the TypeScript core handles the swarms,
            all on one small server.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3>An unconventional choice: µWebSockets.js</h3>
        <p>
          Most Node.js servers, including the reference tracker, handle
          networking with the standard Node.js stack. We took a different route:
          wt-tracker keeps its logic in TypeScript, but hands HTTP, WebSocket
          and TLS handling to{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/uNetworking/uWebSockets.js">
            µWebSockets.js
          </a>
          , a native C++ networking stack. Each connection costs far less CPU
          and memory, and plain (<code>ws://</code>) and secure (
          <code>wss://</code>) connections are served by the same process over
          IPv4 and IPv6.
        </p>

        <h3>Rejecting bad traffic before it costs anything</h3>
        <p>
          Connection limits and allowed-origin checks are applied during the
          HTTP upgrade, before a WebSocket is even created. A malformed message
          closes its connection immediately, so misbehaving clients can't tie up
          the server.
        </p>

        <h3>No unnecessary work per message</h3>
        <p>
          Each peer's state is attached directly to its socket, so incoming
          messages need no lookup to find their sender. Outgoing announce
          responses and offers reuse the same message objects and are serialized
          immediately instead of being allocated for every message. Debug
          logging is checked before any output is built, so it costs nothing
          when turned off.
        </p>

        <h3>Memory designed around the garbage collector</h3>
        <p>
          With tens of thousands of peers joining and leaving, garbage
          collection pauses are a real risk. wt-tracker keeps pools of up to
          100,000 reusable peer and swarm-membership records, so churn recycles
          memory instead of creating garbage. A peer's swarm memberships are a
          linked list stored inside the peer record itself: the common case of a
          peer in a single swarm needs no extra allocation at all.
        </p>

        <h3>Constant-time swarm operations</h3>
        <p>
          Swarms are found by info hash in hash maps, and each peer remembers
          its position in its swarm's list. Removing a peer moves the last peer
          into the freed slot, so leaving a swarm takes the same time whether it
          has ten peers or ten thousand.
        </p>

        <h3>Cheap offer fan-out</h3>
        <p>
          When a peer announces with WebRTC offers, the tracker forwards them to
          other peers in the swarm. If there are enough offers for everyone,
          each peer gets one; otherwise the tracker starts at a random position
          and walks the swarm — no shuffling and no temporary arrays.
        </p>

        <h3>Batched cleanup</h3>
        <p>
          Instead of a timer per peer, one sweep per announce interval removes
          peers that have gone silent for two intervals. The cost of cleanup
          stays flat no matter how many peers are connected.
        </p>

        <h3>Robust and well tested</h3>
        <p>
          100% TypeScript, with CI, unit tests and static analysis on every
          change.
        </p>

        <h2>The results</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>20,000 simultaneous WebSocket Secure peers</strong> on a VPS
            with 1 virtual CPU and 2 GiB of memory — a server that costs a few
            euros a month.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No complex horizontal scaling</strong> for many P2P video
            projects: one small server replaces a cluster.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Private by design:</strong> self-hosting keeps information
            about your audience and P2P network on your own infrastructure.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Production features:</strong> the WebTorrent "scrape"
            extension, live statistics at <code>/stats.json</code>, allowed and
            denied origins, connection limits, and a custom index page.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          For larger audiences, our article{" "}
          <a href="/blog/p2p-video-delivery-for-up-to-100k-viewers-for-free">
            P2P Video Delivery for up to 100k Viewers for Free
          </a>{" "}
          compares tracker options and sizing.
        </p>

        <h2>What's next: every CPU core</h2>
        <p>
          We are working on a multi-core mode. Incoming connections are spread
          across socket worker threads, and swarms are split between tracker
          workers — one per CPU core — by info hash. Each peer talks to the
          worker that owns its swarm over a dedicated message channel, so
          workers never share state or wait on locks.
        </p>

        <h2>What it shows</h2>
        <p>
          wt-tracker is a showcase of how far the Node.js stack can go. Keep the
          business logic in TypeScript, move the networking to native code, and
          design data structures and memory use around the runtime — and one
          small server can take on a load that would otherwise need a cluster.
          The same approach often lets a team stay on Node.js instead of
          rewriting a service in another language or scaling it out.
        </p>

        <h2>Get wt-tracker</h2>
        <p>
          wt-tracker is free and open source under the Apache-2.0 license. It
          works with <a href="/p2p-media-loader/overview">P2P Media Loader</a>{" "}
          and any WebTorrent-compatible client. Installation, configuration and
          deployment are covered in the{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/novage/wt-tracker#readme">
            README on GitHub
          </a>
          .
        </p>

        <LeadBlock title="Need a backend that does more with less?">
          wt-tracker is one example of the performance work we do for clients:
          profiling real traffic, redesigning hot paths and memory use, and
          letting one small server do the job of a cluster — for real-time
          systems, APIs and streaming backends. We also run and scale tracker
          infrastructure for P2P video in production.
        </LeadBlock>
      </div>
    </article>
    <ContactBanner />
  </div>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
